      <h1>Postman Alternatives: The Best API Clients by What You Actually Need</h1>

      <p class="lead">Postman is no longer just an API client. It's a platform with workspaces, mock servers, monitors, Flows, AI credits and an API catalog. If you only used it to send requests and keep a few collections, that's a lot of product to carry, and a good reason to look around.</p>

      <p>This guide groups the alternatives by <strong>the job Postman was doing for you</strong>: sending requests, keeping collections in Git, running tests in CI, and serving mocks. Most "Postman alternatives" lists stop at the first job.</p>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 rounded-lg border border-indigo-200 bg-indigo-50/60 p-6 dark:border-indigo-500/30 dark:bg-indigo-500/5">
        <h2 class="mt-0 text-lg font-semibold text-indigo-900 dark:text-indigo-200">Quick picks</h2>
        <ul class="mb-0 mt-4 space-y-2 text-indigo-900/90 dark:text-indigo-200/90">
          <li><strong>Best free, Git-native client:</strong> <a href="/tools/bruno/" class="text-indigo-600 dark:text-indigo-400">Bruno</a>. Collections are plain files in your repo, and no account is needed.</li>
          <li><strong>Best in the browser, or self-hosted:</strong> <a href="/tools/hoppscotch/" class="text-indigo-600 dark:text-indigo-400">Hoppscotch</a>. MIT-licensed, with a web app and a self-hosted Community Edition.</li>
          <li><strong>Most familiar if you're coming from Postman:</strong> <a href="/tools/insomnia/" class="text-indigo-600 dark:text-indigo-400">Insomnia</a>. A similar desktop app, maintained by Kong.</li>
          <li><strong>Best for the terminal:</strong> <a href="/tools/httpie/" class="text-indigo-600 dark:text-indigo-400">HTTPie</a>. A friendlier curl, plus a desktop/web app in beta.</li>
          <li><strong>Best inside your editor:</strong> Thunder Client, or the free VS Code REST Client extension.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p class="text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong>How we test.</strong> devxtools.dev is independent. We have no affiliate links and no commercial relationship with any vendor on this page. Prices and plans were checked on each vendor's official pricing page on <strong>2026-10-15</strong>. The Bruno and Newman CLI snippets were run on Linux (Node 20.19) against a local test API with Bru CLI 4.2.1 and Newman 6.2.3. Other CLI commands come from each vendor's docs and are labelled that way. Where we quote a vendor's own comparison page, we call it a vendor claim.</p>

      <h2 id="why-developers-look-for-postman-alternatives">Why developers look for Postman alternatives</h2>

      <p>The reasons usually come back to three dated, checkable changes, not to anyone's opinion of Postman.</p>

      <p><strong>An account is needed for most features.</strong> Postman's offline Scratch Pad stopped being available to new downloads on May 15, 2023, and was removed for everyone in the September 15, 2023 release (v10.18). It was replaced by the "lightweight API Client". You can use that without signing in, but Postman's support docs say collections, environments, mocks, monitors and documentation aren't available in it.</p>

      <p><strong>Your data lives in Postman's cloud by default.</strong> Postman describes itself as a cloud platform that syncs your work when you're signed in. To be fair, Postman v12 added <strong>Native Git</strong> on every plan, including Free. It writes collections as multi-file YAML into your repository, so Git storage is no longer something only the alternatives have. Native Git still needs you to be signed in, though.</p>

      <p><strong>The plans changed in March 2026.</strong> On the current pricing page, Free and Solo are <strong>single-user</strong> plans, and collaboration starts on <strong>Team</strong> ($19 per user/month billed annually, $23 monthly). Basic and Professional "are no longer available for new customers". For a small team that used to collaborate on Free or Basic, this is a real cost change.</p>

      <p>None of this makes Postman a bad tool. It means the free, files-on-disk, no-login experience many developers remember now lives elsewhere.</p>

      <h2 id="how-we-evaluated">How we evaluated</h2>

      <p>We asked the same questions of every tool:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Storage.</strong> Where do collections live: local files, Git, or the vendor's cloud? Can you use it with no account (offline or air-gapped)?</li>
        <li><strong>Protocols.</strong> REST, GraphQL, gRPC, WebSocket, and SSE/MQTT where they're listed.</li>
        <li><strong>CI.</strong> Is there a CLI runner that can replace Newman or the Postman CLI, and does it write JUnit?</li>
        <li><strong>Postman import.</strong> Is there a documented path for Collection v2.1 files and environments, and what does it skip?</li>
        <li><strong>Mock servers.</strong> Can it replace Postman's mock servers?</li>
        <li><strong>Licence and price,</strong> from official pages, dated.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Feature lists come from each vendor's pricing page or docs as of 2026-10-15. "Not listed" means we didn't find the feature on an official page. It doesn't prove the feature is missing.</p>

