      <h2 id="the-alternatives">The alternatives</h2>

      <h3 id="bruno">Bruno</h3>

      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> teams who want collections reviewed in pull requests like code.</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Storage:</strong> each request is a plain-text file in a folder you choose. Since <strong>v3.1</strong>, new and imported collections use <strong>OpenCollection YAML</strong>, and the older <code>.bru</code> markup is still supported. Bruno has no cloud component for syncing or storing your work. You share collections through Git.</li>
        <li><strong>CI:</strong> the Bru CLI (<code>npm i -g @usebruno/cli</code>) runs collections and writes JUnit, JSON or HTML reports. We ran <code>bru run --env local --reporter-junit results.xml</code>, and it passed. From CLI v3, scripts run in <strong>Safe Mode</strong> by default. Add <code>--sandbox=developer</code> only for trusted collections that need npm modules or filesystem access. There's also an official GitHub Action (<code>usebruno/bruno-cli-action@v1</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>Import from Postman:</strong> export the collection (v2.1) and environments, then import them in the Bruno app. Common <code>pm.*</code> calls are translated, or you can choose <strong>Preserve scripts</strong> to keep them verbatim. Environment values all become strings. Bulk import of a full Postman data dump is Ultimate-only.</li>
        <li><strong>Mocks:</strong> local <strong>mock servers (beta)</strong>, backed by saved response examples or an OpenAPI spec. They run while the desktop app is open.</li>
        <li><strong>Limits:</strong> on the free edition, the built-in Git UI covers clone, init, diffs and pull. Push, branches, stash and merge-conflict resolution are paid. Free also gets 2 workspaces, public Git providers only, and 5 OpenAPI syncs a month. Secret-manager integrations, SSO, SCIM and audit logs are Ultimate-only.</li>
        <li><strong>Pricing (2026-10-15):</strong> Open Source $0. Pro $6/user/month and Ultimate $11/user/month, billed annually. 14-day Ultimate trial.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>For a deeper head-to-head, see <a href="/comparisons/bruno-vs-postman/">Bruno vs Postman</a>.</p>

      <h3 id="hoppscotch">Hoppscotch</h3>

      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> a fast client that runs in the browser, and teams that want to self-host.</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Storage:</strong> a personal workspace on the free plan. Team workspaces with real-time collaboration start on the $6 plan. Use the cloud version or self-host the Community Edition.</li>
        <li><strong>CI:</strong> the Hoppscotch CLI is labelled <strong>alpha</strong> in its docs. Install it with <code>npm i -g @hoppscotch/cli</code>, then run <code>hopp test collection.json --env env.json</code>. The docs list JUnit report output.</li>
        <li><strong>Import from Postman:</strong> the importer accepts Postman Collection v2.0/v2.1 and Postman environments. Script import is <strong>experimental and opt-in</strong> (from v2025.10.0). Hoppscotch lists <code>pm.visualizer</code>, <code>pm.collectionVariables</code>, <code>pm.iterationData</code>, <code>setNextRequest</code> and <code>require</code> as unsupported, and advanced auth settings aren't imported.</li>
        <li><strong>Mocks:</strong> "API mocking" is listed on the $6/user plan.</li>
        <li><strong>Licence:</strong> MIT.</li>
        <li><strong>Pricing (2026-10-15, cloud):</strong> $0. Business $6/user/month and Enterprise $11/user/month (SSO, SCIM, audit logs), billed annually.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="insomnia">Insomnia</h3>

      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Postman users who want a familiar desktop app with more storage choices.</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Storage:</strong> local, cloud, or Git Sync projects. On the free Essentials plan, Git Sync is unlimited for up to 3 users, and cloud/local projects are unlimited. Without an account you get the Scratch Pad only. Local Vault projects also need a (free) account. End-to-end encryption is listed on every plan.</li>
        <li><strong>Protocols:</strong> the pricing page lists REST, gRPC, GraphQL, SOAP, WebSocket, Socket.IO and SSE clients.</li>
        <li><strong>CI:</strong> the Inso CLI is included on the free plan. A collection run from the docs looks like this:
          <pre><code class="language-bash">inso run collection "My Collection" --env "CI" --ci</code></pre>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Mocks:</strong> a hosted mock server with 1,000 requests/month on Essentials and 10,000 on Pro, then $10 per 25,000 requests. Unlimited on Enterprise.</li>
        <li><strong>Licence:</strong> Apache-2.0.</li>
        <li><strong>Pricing (2026-10-15):</strong> Essentials $0. Pro $12/user/month. Enterprise $45/user/month, self-serve up to 50 users (SSO, SCIM, vault integrations).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="httpie">HTTPie</h3>

      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> developers who live in the terminal.</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>CLI:</strong> <code>http GET api.example.com/users</code> with readable, coloured output and sensible JSON defaults. It's open source under BSD-3-Clause. Note that the CLI repository's last push was in December 2024 (GitHub API, checked 2026-10-15).</li>
        <li><strong>App:</strong> HTTPie for Desktop and Web is labelled a <strong>public beta</strong>. HTTPie says you can use it offline and without an account, organise work in spaces and collections, and sync across devices.</li>
        <li><strong>Limits:</strong> no collection runner aimed at CI and no mock server. On 2026-10-15 we found no public pricing page (httpie.io/pricing returned 404).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="yaak">Yaak</h3>

      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> individuals and small teams who want a fast, local-first desktop client with files in Git.</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Storage:</strong> local-first. Yaak stores one YAML file per request, folder and environment, and it has Git sync with a built-in Git UI. Yaak says it doesn't store or sync your data on its servers.</li>
        <li><strong>Protocols:</strong> HTTP, GraphQL, gRPC, WebSocket and Server-Sent Events, according to yaak.app.</li>
        <li><strong>Import:</strong> Postman collections and environments, Insomnia, Bruno, OpenAPI/Swagger and curl.</li>
        <li><strong>CLI:</strong> Yaak ships a CLI.</li>
        <li><strong>Licence and price:</strong> the source is MIT. A licence is required to use the prebuilt app <strong>at work</strong>, and personal use is free. Individual $79/year or $399 one-time. Business $149/user/year. 30-day trial. The latest release at the time of writing was v2026.9.0 (October 10, 2026).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="thunder-client-and-vs-code-rest-client">Thunder Client and the VS Code REST Client</h3>

      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> staying in your editor.</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Thunder Client</strong> is a VS Code extension, with JetBrains support on paid plans. Collection data is stored locally on your machine. Git Sync, CLI and CI/CD, and WebSocket/SSE/gRPC are listed on paid plans, and the free plan has usage limits. <strong>Pricing (2026-10-15):</strong> Free, Starter $3, Business $7 and Enterprise $16 per user/month.</li>
        <li><strong>REST Client</strong> (by Huachao Mao, MIT) takes another approach. You write requests in <code>.http</code> or <code>.rest</code> files and send them from the editor:
          <pre><code class="language-http">{`@baseUrl = http://localhost:4173

### List users
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/users?page=1
Accept: application/json`}</code></pre>
          The files live in your repo and review cleanly. There's no runner or assertions, so pair it with real tests.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="apidog">Apidog</h3>

      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> teams who want an all-in-one design, test, mock and docs platform and don't mind it being cloud-first.</p>

      <p>Apidog publishes a lot of comparison content about Postman alternatives. Treat that as vendor marketing, not as a neutral source. Its pricing page lists local and cloud mock servers and an Apidog CLI. Multiple paid plans available (see apidog.com/pricing for current rates). 14-day trial.</p>

      <h3 id="curl-http-files">curl + .http files (the minimalist option)</h3>

      <p>If you mostly replay a handful of requests, you may not need an app. Keep <code>curl</code> commands or <code>.http</code> files in the repo next to the code, review them in PRs, and run your real API tests in your test framework. There's nothing to install or license, and nothing to migrate later. The trade-off is that you get no GUI, no environments UI, and no collection runner.</p>

      <h2 id="replacing-postman-other-jobs">Replacing Postman's other jobs</h2>

      <h3 id="running-collections-in-ci">Running collections in CI</h3>

      <p>Newman still runs <strong>exported Collection v2.1</strong> files, so it works as a stopgap while you migrate. It <strong>can't run Collection v3</strong> (Postman v12 Native Git YAML). For v3, Postman's docs point you to <code>postman collection run</code>, which supports only the <code>cli</code> reporter for YAML collections.</p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Tool</th>
              <th>Install</th>
              <th>Run</th>
              <th>JUnit report</th>
              <th>Source</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Postman (Newman)</td>
              <td><code>npm i -g newman</code></td>
              <td><code>newman run postman_collection.json -e local.postman_environment.json</code></td>
              <td><code>-r cli,junit --reporter-junit-export out.xml</code></td>
              <td>Run by us</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Postman CLI</td>
              <td>See Postman docs</td>
              <td><code>postman collection run &lt;path-or-id&gt;</code></td>
              <td>Not for v3 YAML collections</td>
              <td>Docs</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Bruno</td>
              <td><code>npm i -g @usebruno/cli</code></td>
              <td><code>bru run --env local</code> (in the collection folder)</td>
              <td><code>--reporter-junit out.xml</code></td>
              <td>Run by us</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Hoppscotch</td>
              <td><code>npm i -g @hoppscotch/cli</code></td>
              <td><code>hopp test collection.json --env env.json</code></td>
              <td>Not listed</td>
              <td>Docs</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Insomnia</td>
              <td>Inso CLI (Kong docs)</td>
              <td><code>inso run collection "&lt;name&gt;" --env "&lt;env&gt;" --ci</code></td>
              <td>Not listed</td>
              <td>Docs</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>A minimal GitHub Actions job for Bruno:</p>

      <pre><code class="language-yaml">{`jobs:
  api-tests:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with: { node-version: 22 }
      - run: npm i -g @usebruno/cli
      - run: bru run --env ci --reporter-junit results.xml
        working-directory: ./api-collection`}</code></pre>

      <h3 id="replacing-postman-mock-servers">Replacing Postman mock servers</h3>

      <p>Most lists skip this. If your frontend or E2E tests point at a Postman mock URL, you need a replacement before you cancel:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong><a href="/tools/mockoon/">Mockoon</a>:</strong> a desktop app and CLI for local mock APIs. It's a good fit if you liked clicking routes together in Postman. The CLI runs the same data file in CI: <code>npx @mockoon/cli start --data ./mocks.json --port 3000</code>. It also accepts an OpenAPI file, and there's a Docker image.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="/tools/wiremock/">WireMock</a>:</strong> a JVM-based mock server with request matching, stateful scenarios and record/playback. It runs well in Docker and CI.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="/tools/prism/">Prism</a></strong> (by <a href="/tools/stoplight/">Stoplight</a>): serves mock responses straight from an OpenAPI document, so your mocks follow the spec. We ran <code>prism mock openapi.yaml</code> (prism-cli 5.16.0; Prism 5.16.0 requires Node 24.18+ per its engines field), and it served our example on port 4010.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="/tools/msw/">MSW</a>:</strong> if the mocks are only for your own frontend tests, mock at the network layer in the app instead of running a server.</li>
        <li><strong>Bruno's local mock servers (beta)</strong>, <strong>Insomnia's mocks</strong> and <strong>Hoppscotch's API mocking</strong> also cover this job if you're already using one of those clients.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Compare these with what Postman offers on our <a href="/tools/postman-mock-servers/">Postman mock servers</a> page.</p>

      <h3 id="monitors-and-contract-checks">Monitors and contract checks</h3>

      <p>Postman monitors run collections on a schedule, and the free plan includes 1,000 monitoring requests a month. No open-source client on this list has a hosted monitor built in. The usual replacement is a scheduled CI job (for example, a GitHub Actions <code>schedule:</code> trigger) that runs your CLI collection and alerts on failure. To check that an API still matches its contract, look at Pact (consumer-driven contracts) or Schemathesis (property-based tests from an OpenAPI schema). Those deserve their own guides.</p>

      <h2 id="migrating-from-postman">Migrating from Postman step by step</h2>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Export collections</strong> as <strong>Collection v2.1</strong> JSON from Postman. If you've already moved to Native Git, check which formats your target tool accepts first, because most importers document v2.x.</li>
        <li><strong>Export environments</strong> separately. They aren't included in the collection file. Before you commit anything, strip secrets or replace them with placeholders.</li>
        <li><strong>Import</strong> into your new tool using its documented importer.</li>
        <li><strong>Fix scripts.</strong> Pre-request and test scripts that use the <code>pm.*</code> API are where migrations break. Bruno translates common calls. Hoppscotch's script import is experimental and skips several APIs. Expect to edit some scripts by hand.</li>
        <li><strong>Fix auth.</strong> Check OAuth 2.0 flows, collection-level auth and inherited auth on every folder. Hoppscotch, for example, doesn't import advanced auth settings.</li>
        <li><strong>Commit to Git</strong> (for file-based tools). Add environment files that hold secrets to <code>.gitignore</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Swap CI</strong> from Newman to the new tool's CLI, and run both side by side for a week.</li>
        <li><strong>Replace mocks and monitors</strong> before you turn off the Postman workspace.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="when-postman-is-still-right">When Postman is still the right choice</h2>

      <p>Postman is still a good default if you depend on <strong>hosted mock servers and scheduled monitors</strong>, if collaborators <strong>don't use Git</strong> (PMs, QA, partners who need a shared workspace and a link), or if you use its documentation publishing, API catalog, AI features (Agent Mode, metered in AI credits) or governance features. Paying $19 per user/month for that integration can cost less than assembling it from four tools.</p>

      <h2 id="which-should-you-choose">Which Postman alternative should you choose?</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>If you…</th>
              <th>Choose</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>want collections in Git, reviewed in PRs, with no cloud and no account</td>
