      <p>Not sure yet? Try the <a href="/picker/">tool picker</a> or browse more <a href="/comparisons/">comparisons</a> in our <a href="/api-development/">API development hub</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>

      <h3>What is the best free alternative to Postman?</h3>
      <p>For most developers, Bruno: the core is MIT-licensed, it's free to use at work, and collections are local files you can keep in Git. Hoppscotch is the best free option if you want a browser-based client.</p>

      <h3>Is there an open-source Postman alternative?</h3>
      <p>Yes. Bruno (MIT), Hoppscotch (MIT), Insomnia (Apache-2.0) and the HTTPie CLI (BSD-3-Clause) are open source. Yaak's source is MIT, but it asks for a paid licence for its prebuilt app when used at work.</p>

      <h3>Is Bruno free for commercial use?</h3>
      <p>Yes. The open-source edition is MIT-licensed, and MIT allows commercial use. The paid Pro and Ultimate tiers add features such as Git push and branching in the built-in UI, private Git providers, more workspaces, SSO/SCIM and support. You don't need them to use Bruno at work.</p>

      <h3>What is the best Postman alternative for Mac / VS Code?</h3>
      <p>All the desktop tools here run on macOS. If you want to stay in VS Code, use Thunder Client (it has a free tier, and paid plans add CLI, Git Sync and more protocols) or the free REST Client extension with <code>.http</code> files.</p>

      <h3>Can I import my Postman collections into Bruno or Insomnia?</h3>
      <p>Yes. Both document importing exported Postman collections. Environments are exported and imported separately, and scripts using the <code>pm.*</code> API may need manual fixes.</p>

      <h3>What replaces Postman mock servers?</h3>
      <p>Mockoon, WireMock and Prism are the common standalone replacements, and MSW covers frontend tests. Bruno (beta), Insomnia, Hoppscotch's paid plan and Apidog also have built-in mocking.</p>

